// A test case's literals are in the node store, and are not expressions of the
// program. They are built at the type the signature declares -- which a list
// literal on its own does not know, so `[]` and `[1000000000000, 2]` would be
// judged here as an empty list with no context and a list of mixed types. The
// case checks judge them instead, against the signature
// (mid/names/limits/shape/cases/more/fit/).
//
// A case's nodes are created after the node of the function it is written
// under and before its own "case" node, so a node lies strictly between the
// two exactly when it belongs to a case. Only list literals are asked: they
// are the one kind the checks here could misjudge, and they are rare.

tc_node_at(int id) {
  if(k_of(id) != "arr" || in_case(id) == 0) {
    tc_node(id);
  }
} return void;
